course: dictionary
lesson: 5640
title: A shared language
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word lingua comes from Latin, where it means tongue and language. Many English words come from Latin, so knowing this one helps you guess related words. «A lingua franca is a shared language used between people who speak different first languages.» The clue is the word shared: people use it so they can understand each other.
explain: Read the second example: «When people with different first languages need to trade or rule, they use a shared one, a lingua franca, like Swahili on the East African coast.» Trade means buying and selling, so people from different places need one common language to talk to each other. Try your meaning in the sentence: a shared language to help people trade still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: In English, lingua is mostly found in the phrase lingua franca. Words built from it, like linguist and bilingual, show the link. A linguist studies languages, and bilingual means speaking two languages.
fact: Lingua is the Latin word for tongue and language.
fact: A lingua franca is a shared language used by people who speak different first languages.
fact: «Swahili on the East African coast» is an example of a lingua franca.
fact: «Persian at the courts of India» is another example, where people with different first languages used one shared language.
fact: In medicine, lingua is a rare word for the tongue.
exercise: «A lingua franca is a shared language used between people who speak different first languages.» What is a lingua franca? | A Latin sentence written on a wall | A type of tongue twister | A language only one family speaks at home | *A shared language that people with different first languages use to understand each other
exercise: Which word in the lessons means speaking two languages? | treacherous | famished | *bilingual | curt
exercise: In the examples, where is Swahili used as a lingua franca? | At the courts of India | In Scotland, for grey wet days | In Australia, at the petrol station | *On the East African coast
test: What does lingua mean in Latin? | *Tongue and language | Bridge | Court | Trade
test: Why do people use a lingua franca? | *So people who speak different first languages can understand each other | To write poems in secret | To make a noisy market | To learn Latin only
exam: Which sentence best explains lingua franca? | A language used for tea parties | A word for a tongue twister | A language only children speak at school | *A shared language used by people who speak different first languages
